+It asks GitHub for the newest release, through `gh` when it is signed in and anonymously otherwise, downloads that release's `install.sh`, and runs it for that release's tag. It then runs the new `~/.local/bin/trip --version`, and if that does not report the release's version, it prints both and exits 1. By then the installer has already made that build `current`, so the message says so and, when the installer kept a previous version, gives the command that switches back to it, such as `ln -sfn 0.2.0 ~/.local/share/trip/current`. It upgrades from the repository the install came from, which `~/.local/share/trip/release.json` records, so a fork upgrades from the fork. To see whether there is anything to upgrade to, without installing anything, run `trip upgrade --check`.
 
